public with sharing class CaseSurveyJob implements Queueable {

    private final Set<Id> caseIds;

    // Backwards-compatible single-case constructor.
    public CaseSurveyJob(Id caseId) {
        this.caseIds = new Set<Id>{ caseId };
    }

    // Bulk constructor used by the trigger.
    public CaseSurveyJob(Set<Id> caseIds) {
        this.caseIds = caseIds == null ? new Set<Id>() : caseIds;
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (caseIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Re-query so the job always works from current data and has the
        // fields it needs (CaseNumber, ContactId).
        List<Case> cases = [
            SELECT Id, CaseNumber, ContactId
            FROM Case
            WHERE Id IN :caseIds
        ];

        List<Task> tasksToInsert = new List<Task>();

        for (Case c : cases) {
            // Defensive: the contact could have been cleared between enqueue
            // and execution.
            if (c.ContactId == null) {
                continue;
            }

            tasksToInsert.add(new Task(
                WhatId       = c.Id,
                WhoId        = c.ContactId,
                Subject      = 'CSAT survey: ' + c.CaseNumber,
                ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(2),
                Status       = 'Not Started'
            ));
        }

        if (tasksToInsert.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Partial-success insert so one bad record can't block the rest,
        // keeping the original "one failure can't affect the others" goal.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results =
            Database.insert(tasksToInsert, false);

        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                for (Database.Error err : results[i].getErrors()) {
                    System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                        'CSAT task insert failed for ' +
                        tasksToInsert[i].WhatId + ': ' + err.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Explanation of what changed and why:

- **The failure.** The platform allows at most **50 `System.enqueueJob` calls per transaction**. The previous "one job per case" design threw `Too many queueable jobs added to the queue: 51` the moment the nightly cleanup closed more than 50 qualifying cases in a single API call (`nightlyCleanupOf200`). That design simply cannot scale past 50 per transaction.

- **The fix.** The trigger now collects all newly-closed, contact-bearing case Ids into a `Set<Id>` and enqueues **one** `CaseSurveyJob` for the whole batch — a single `enqueueJob` call regardless of how many cases close, so it is safe for the 200-at-a-time nightly job and for bulk list-view closures.

- **Preserving failure isolation.** The original requirement was that one failure can't affect the others. Instead of one job per case, the job now re-queries all cases, builds the tasks in bulk, and inserts them with `Database.insert(tasks, false)` (partial success / `allOrNone = false`). A single bad record is logged and skipped while every other survey task is still created — the same isolation guarantee, but within platform limits and with just one SOQL and one DML.

- **Compatibility.** I kept a single-Id constructor as well as the new `Set<Id>` constructor, so any existing callers (or tests) using `new CaseSurveyJob(c.Id)` still work.

